== Marital Love and Self-Love

=== §. 53. #emph[Of the application to husbands of the things that Christ has done for the church.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:28.]

    #text(style: "italic")[So men ought to love their wives, etc.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] first clause of this verse serves both as an #emph[application] of the former argument and as a #emph[transition] to another argument.

The particle of relation (So) shows that what has been said before of Christ’s love to his church ought to be referred and applied to husbands. For as Christ loved his church, #emph[so] husbands ought to love their wives.

#emph[Question.] Why are these transcendent evidences of Christ’s surpassing love to his church set before husbands? Can any such things be expected from husbands toward their wives?

#emph[Answer.] No, #footnote[See §. 27.] not in measure, but in likeness. For in this large declaration of Christ’s love there are two general points to be noted.

1\. That the church in herself was in no way worthy of love.

2\. That Christ so carried himself toward her that he made her worthy of much love.

This ought to be the mind of husbands toward their wives.

1\. Though their wives are in no way worthy of love, yet husbands must love them.

2\. They must endeavor, with all the understanding and wisdom they have, to make them worthy of love. I say #emph[endeavor] because it is not wholly in the husband’s power to do the deed. Yet his faithful endeavor shall, on his part, be accepted for the deed.

I shall speak more fully of these points hereafter.

=== §. 54. #emph[Of the application to a husband of the love that a man bears to himself.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:28.]

    #text(style: "italic")[So men ought to love their wives as their own bodies.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] forenamed particle (So) also has relation to another pattern, namely, that of a man’s self to his body. And so it is a transition from one argument to another.

There is more emphasis used here in setting down a husband’s duty than there was before, in v. 25.

There it was laid down by way of exhortation: “Husbands, love your#super[+] wives.”

Here it is laid down with a stricter charge: #emph[husbands ought to love their wives.] So this duty is not an optional matter, left to the husband’s will to do or to leave undone. A necessity is laid upon him: he must love his wife. Woe to him, therefore, if he does not do it.

In setting down this argument taken from a man’s self, the apostle likens a man’s wife to his #emph[body.] In this he has relation to v. 23, where he said, “the husband is the head of the wife.” By this he shows that as a husband’s place is a motive to his wife to perform her duty, so it is to himself, #footnote[See #emph[Treatise] 4, §. 74, 76. Why the pattern of a man’s self is added to the pattern of Christ.] to perform his duty.

He is her head; therefore she must be subject to him.

She is his body; therefore he must love her.

This example of a man’s self is both a #emph[reason,] to move husbands the more to love their wives, and also a #emph[rule,] to teach them how to love them.

The #emph[reason] is implied in that near union which is between #footnote[#emph[Sicut corpus nunquam à se dissidet, neque anima aduersus seipsum, ita virum & mulierem non conuenit dissidere. Chrys. in Gen. hom.] 45.] a man and his wife: she is as near to him as his own body; therefore she ought to be as dear to him. The body never disagrees with itself, nor the soul with itself. So neither should man and wife.

The #emph[rule] is noted in the manner of a man’s loving his own body: as entirely as he loves his body, so entirely he ought to love his wife.

Of the manner of a man’s loving himself, see Treatise 4, §. 74, 76.

To enforce this comparison the more, the apostle adds, “He who loves his wife loves himself.” #footnote[Eph 5:28.]

Two things are implied by this clause.

1\. That a wife is not only as a man’s body, namely, his outward flesh, but as his person, his body and soul. She is as #emph[his body] because she was taken out of his body,#footnote[Gen 2:23.] and because she is set under him as his body is under his head. She is as #emph[himself] by reason of the bond of marriage, which makes #emph[one] of #emph[two.]#footnote[Matt 19:5, 6.] In this respect a wife is commonly called a man’s #emph[second self.]

2\. That a husband in loving his wife loves himself, so that the benefit of loving his wife will redound to himself as well as to his wife.

=== §. 55. #emph[Of the amplification of a man’s love of himself.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:29.]

    #text(style: "italic")[For no man ever yet hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it, just as the Lord does the church.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] former pattern of a man’s self is further amplified here. For first the apostle proves that a man loves himself, and then he shows how he loves himself.

Two arguments are used to prove the point.

One is taken from the contrary: #emph[No man ever yet hated his own flesh. Therefore he loves it.]

The other is taken from the effects of love: #emph[To nourish and cherish one’s flesh is a fruit of love. But every man nourishes and cherishes his flesh. Therefore he loves it.]

This latter argument shows the manner of a man’s loving himself, and in this a man’s love of himself is a rule to teach him how to love his wife.

This indefinite phrase (#emph[no man]) is to be restricted to such as have the understanding and feeling of a man in them, as if he had said, #emph[no man in his right mind.] For furious, frantic, mad, desperate persons will cut their arms, legs and other parts, mangle their flesh, hang, drown, smother, choke and stab themselves. Even so, those who #footnote[#emph[Quia se diligat, & prodesse sibi velit, dubitare dementis est. Aug. de doct. Christ. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 25.] hate their wives, or hurt them in any way, are as men out of their minds. Indeed, it is the part of a madman to doubt that he loves himself and does good to himself.

These two words (to #emph[nourish] and #emph[cherish]) comprise under them a diligent providing of all things needful for a man’s body.

To #emph[nourish] is properly to feed. #footnote[ἐκτρέφει.]

To #emph[cherish] is to keep warm. #footnote[θάλπει.]

The former is done by #emph[food,] the latter by #emph[clothing.] Under #footnote[Proprie dicitur de galina fouente oua, quum illis incubat. \[It is properly said of a hen warming her eggs, when she sits on them.\]] food and clothing the apostle comprises all things needful for this life, where he says, “If we have food and clothing, let us be content with these.” #footnote[1 Tim 6:8.]

Applied to a husband, this shows that he ought to have a provident care for the good of his wife in all things needful for her.

To press this point yet further, he returns again to the example of Christ (“just as the Lord does the church”). The apostle thought that this nail of love needed to be driven fast into the heads and hearts of husbands. Therefore he adds blow to blow to knock it in deep, even to the head. Before, he confirmed Christ’s example with the example of ourselves; here he confirms the example of ourselves with the example of Christ again. He does this for two special reasons.

1\. To urge the point more forcibly. For two examples add weight to one another, especially this latter one, which is so far more excellent, as we heard from v. 25, 26, 27.

2\. To give husbands better direction for their provident care toward their wives. They must nourish and cherish them not only as their own bodies, but as Christ nourishes and cherishes his church: not only with things temporal, but also with things spiritual and eternal.

=== §. 56. #emph[Of man’s natural affection toward himself.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:28, 29.]

    #text(style: "italic")[So men ought to love their wives as their own bodies. He who loves his wife loves himself. For no man ever yet hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it, just as the Lord does the church.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[Having] briefly shown the general scope of the 28th and 29th verses, I will proceed to handle them more distinctly.

They set forth #emph[the natural affection of a man to himself.] #footnote[The sum of v. 28 and 29.]

Two points are to be noted here.

1\. The general proposition, #emph[that a man is well disposed toward himself.]

2\. The particular amplification and manifestation of that affection.

This is manifested in two ways.

Negatively: #emph[No man hates his own flesh.] Affirmatively, and that in two branches: he #emph[nourishes] and #emph[cherishes] it.

Both these are justified by the like affection of Christ to the church, which is his body (“just as the Lord does the church”). In that the apostle sets forth the natural affection of a man’s self to his body as a motive and pattern to Christians to love their wives, and also justifies it by a like affection of Christ to his church, I observe that

#emph[Natural affection is a thing lawful and commendable.] It is #footnote[Doctrine.] an affection which may stand with a good conscience. God’s word is so far from taking it away that it establishes it. For such as are #footnote[ἄστοργοι.] “without natural affection”#footnote[2 Tim 3:3.] are directly condemned,#footnote[Rom 1:31.] and we are #footnote[Rom 12:10.] commanded to be as tenderly affectionate toward one another as #footnote[φιλόστοργοι] we are toward ourselves. Indeed, the law in its strict rigor lays down the natural affection which is in a man toward himself as a rule for the love of his neighbor#footnote[Matt 22:39.] (“Love your neighbor as yourself”). Hence it is that the #footnote[Lev 19:18. Isa 58:7.] prophets, #footnote[1 Cor 12:26. Jas 2:8.] apostles, and Christ himself often call on us to have an eye to the affection which we bear to ourselves. Of this pattern Christ says, “This is the Law and the Prophets”:#footnote[Matt 7:12.] this is the brief sum of them; this is what they much urge and press.

1\. Natural affection was at first created by God, and #footnote[Reasons.] planted by him in man. So, as the soul and the body, and their powers and parts, are good things in their substance, this affection also is good in itself.

2\. There are the same reasons to love ourselves as to love our brethren. For we ourselves are made in God’s image, redeemed by Christ’s blood, members of the same mystical body, and keepers of ourselves, who must give an account of the good or hurt we do to ourselves, and the like. In the law, under this word #emph[neighbor,] we ourselves are included, and every commandment of the second table is to be applied to ourselves.

=== §. 57. #emph[Of natural self-love.]

#emph[Objection.] Lovers of themselves are condemned in God’s word, as 2 Tim 3:2, Phil 2:21, 1 Cor 10:24, Rom 15:1.

#emph[Answer.] There is a double loving of a man’s self. #footnote[The distinct kinds of self-love.] One is good and commendable; the other is evil and damnable.

The good and commendable loving of a man’s self is

+ Natural.
+ Spiritual.

That which is natural is in all men by the very instinct of nature. It was created at first, and is always preserved in our nature by God’s providence, and that for the preservation of nature. If there were not such a natural love of himself in every one, a man would be as careless of himself as of others, and as unwilling to take pains for himself as for others. Therefore, so that every one might have care of at least one, namely himself, and so the world be better preserved, God has reserved in man this natural affection, notwithstanding his corruption by sin. Yes, further: because not every one is able to look after himself, at least when he is young, sick, old, or in any other way helpless, God by his wise providence has extended this natural affection toward others also, as they are closely linked to us by the bonds of nature. The nearest to a man’s self (by blood and the bond of nature) are his children. It is wonderful how much parents do for their children, which they would never do if there were not a natural affection in them toward their children. From children again this affection rises toward their parents, so that when parents grow old, helpless, or in any way unable to help themselves, they might have relief from their children. And because parents and children are not always together, or are not able to help one another, or are unnatural, God has extended this natural affection yet further, to brothers, kinsmen and other relatives. And to extend it further still, he has instituted marriage between those who are not of the same blood, and by virtue of that bond has raised a natural affection not only in husband and wife toward one another, but also in all the kindred by marriage that is made by it. Moreover, this affection is wrought in neighbors, friends, companions and others knit together by like bonds, so that the bow of God’s providence might have many strings, and if one breaks, another might hold. In all these kinds, the nearer a man comes to himself, the more this affection shows itself, according to the proverb, #emph[Near is my coat, but nearer is my skin.] Since God has wrought this natural affection in its distinct kinds, and there are good ends and uses of it, it is not to be condemned.

=== §. 58. #emph[Of spiritual self-love.]

Spiritual self-love is that which is wrought in man supernaturally by God’s Spirit. By it he is both enlightened to discern what is most excellent and best for him, and also moved to choose it, so that this serves to rectify the former. Hence it comes about that the chief care of such men is for their souls and for their eternal salvation. To further it they can be content, as need requires, to #footnote[1 Cor 9:27.] beat down their bodies, to #footnote[2 Cor 11:27.] deny them at times their ordinary refreshment by food, rest and other like means, and even to #footnote[Heb 11:36, 37.] allow them to be imprisoned, racked and otherwise tortured, and life itself to be taken from them. Men do and suffer this, #footnote[#emph[Non propterea quisquam dicendus est non diligere salutem, atque incolumitatem corporis sui, quia amplius aliquid diligit: nam auarus etc. Aug. de doct. Chr. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 25.] not for want of natural affection, but by reason of a spiritual affection which persuades them that it is good for them that it should be so. A man is not therefore to be said not to love the health and safety of his body because he loves something more. For though a covetous man loves his money, yet he can be content to part with it for bread to nourish his body. So though a spiritual man loves his life, yet he can be content to lose it for his soul’s salvation. For he loves himself sufficiently who does his best to enjoy the chief and truest good. This spiritual affection extends itself as #footnote[#emph[Ille se satis diligit qui sedulo agit vt summo & vero perfruatur bono. Aug. de mor. eccl. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 26.] far as natural affection, namely to wives, husbands, children, parents, brothers, kinsmen, friends, etc. It is much urged and pressed in the Scriptures, as Isa 55:1, 2, 3; Matt 6:19, 20, 33; John 6:27; 1 Tim 6:11, 19.

=== §. 59. #emph[Of evil self-love.]

The self-love which is evil swerves in its

- #emph[Object.]
- #emph[Measure.]

1\. In the #emph[object,] when it is set on our corruptions, our lusts, our evil inclinations: when we desire and love them, and for their sake pursue whatever may satisfy them, as the ambitious, lustful, dissolute, gluttonous and other such persons do. This is expressly forbidden: “Make no provision for the flesh, to gratify its lusts.” #footnote[Rom 13:14.]

2\. In the #emph[measure,] when our love is wholly and only set on ourselves, so seeking our own good that we regard no man’s good but our own, nor care what damage another receives, so long as we may gain advantage by it. This is also forbidden, #footnote[Phil 2:21. 1 Cor 13:5; 10:24.] for it is contrary to the property of true love, which “does not seek its own,” namely to the hurt of another. This has the title of #footnote[φιλαυτία.] #emph[self-love] appropriated to it. It sprang from the corruption of nature, and is daily increased by the instigation of Satan for the destruction of mankind. It shows itself in the many tricks of deceit which most men use in their dealings with others; in taking advantage of others’ necessities, as in the case of usury, and of raising the price of grain and other goods in time of scarcity, and the like; in men’s slowness to help those who stand in need of their help; in want of compassion for other men’s miseries; and in many other such unkindnesses. All of these verify the proverb, #emph[Every man for himself.]

But by distinguishing the forenamed points we may see that, although #emph[evil self-love] is a most detestable vice, yet it is both lawful and commendable to love oneself rightly.

=== §. 60. #emph[Of the error of the Stoics in condemning all passion.]

The folly of the Stoics, who would have all natural affection #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] rooted out of man, is contrary to this pattern, and unworthy to find any welcome among Christians. For what do they aim at but to root out of man what God has planted in him, and to take away the means which God has used for the better preservation of man? That wise man whom they frame for themselves is worse than a brute beast: he is a mere log or block. Not only the best and wisest men that ever were in the world, but also Christ himself had in him those passions and affections which they account unbecoming a wise man. Their folly has long since been hissed out of the schools of the philosophers; should it then find a place in Christ’s church?

=== §. 61. #emph[Of using natural affection well.]

Let us labor to cherish this natural affection in us, and to #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] turn it to the best things: even to such as are good not only in appearance but in truth, and among good things to such as are most excellent and most necessary, such as concern our souls and eternal life. To this end we must pray to have our understandings enlightened (that we may “#emph[discern] #footnote[Phil 1:10.] what differs, and approve what is excellent”), and to have our wills and affections sanctified, so that we embrace, pursue and delight in what we know to be best. So our #emph[natural] affection will be turned into a #emph[spiritual] affection.

Here we see how we may make nature a schoolmaster to us. For as Christ sends us to the birds of the air and the lilies #footnote[Matt 6:26, 28.] of the field to learn from them, so the apostle here sends us to our own natural instinct. We cannot complain that we have no schoolmaster near us (as many in the country can, whose children are brought up untaught for want of one): we ourselves are schoolmasters to ourselves. Therefore, as the apostle by this teaches husbands to love their wives, so let us all learn more generally to love one another. For #footnote[1 Cor 12:12.] we are all mutual members of one and the same body, and our brother or neighbor is “our own flesh.”#footnote[Isa 58:7.]

=== §. 62. #emph[Of man’s forbearing to wrong himself.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:29.]

    #text(style: "italic")[For no man ever yet hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] first particle (#emph[for]) shows that in this verse an evidence and manifestation of a man’s love of himself is given. Its first part, which is set down negatively, shows that

#emph[It is against the common instinct of nature for a man to hate] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[himself.] It is noted as an evidence that devils were in the Gadarene, in that “he was cutting himself with stones.”#footnote[Mark 5:5.] Had the devils not forced him, he would never have done it.

Hatred is contrary to love. Since it was proved before #footnote[Reason.] that every man by nature loves himself, it follows by necessary consequence that no man hates his flesh. For two contrary effects do not proceed from the same cause: “no spring can yield both salt water and fresh.” #footnote[Jas 3:12.]

#emph[Objection.] Many waste their bodies with fasts, vigils, labors, toils and the like. Others tear and gash their flesh with whips, knives, swords, and even with their teeth. Others lay such violent hands on themselves that they take away their own lives.

#emph[Answer.] 1. None of these things are done by the instinct of nature which God has set in man, but through the corruption of nature which the devil has caused. Now nature and the corruption of nature are two contrary causes; no wonder, then, that contrary effects come from them.

2\. They think they do these things in love to themselves: superstitious persons, to merit salvation by wasting their bodies; others, to free themselves from disgrace, poverty, slavery, torment or such evils. So there is an apparent good that makes them do it, and not simply hatred of themselves. Those who do so are either possessed with a devil, or blinded in their minds, or bereft of their wits, or overwhelmed with some passion, so that they do not know what they do. Therefore they do not do it in hatred.

2\. #emph[Objection.] Holy and wise men, deliberately and on good advice, have “beaten down their bodies,” and yielded their lives #footnote[1 Cor 9:27. Heb 11:35. #emph[etc.]] to be taken away, “refusing their release.”

#emph[Answer.] That was far from hatred, and done in great love to themselves, as was shown #footnote[§. 58.] before.

=== §. 63. #emph[Of unnatural practices against oneself.]

The forenamed doctrine exposes many practices used #footnote[1 #emph[Use.]] by various men as being against nature, and in that respect most horrible and detestable.

1\. #emph[The practice of the idolatrous Baalites,] who, to move their idol to hear them, “cut themselves with knives and lances … until the blood gushed over them.”#footnote[1 Kgs 18:28. #emph[Cassian. Collat.] 19.] Not much unlike them are popish hermits, anchorites, flagellant monks, Grandimontenses, various sorts of Franciscans, and other friars. Of these #footnote[#emph[D. Willet Synops. Papism. Contr.] 6. #emph[quaest.] 6.] some wear shirts of haircloth, some shirts of mail next to their bodies, some go barefoot, some whip themselves daily until blood follows, and some waste their bodies with lying hard, vigils, fasting, going on pilgrimage, etc.

2\. #emph[The practice of gluttons, drunkards, unchaste and pleasure-loving persons,] who, to satisfy their corrupt appetites, impair their health, bring diseases on themselves and shorten their days.

3\. #emph[The practice of brawlers,] who by quarrels cause their flesh to be wounded and their lives taken away. Among these may be reckoned those who bring themselves into great straits, distresses and dangers for the sake of gain, and those who by felony, treason and like evil deeds cast themselves on the sword of the magistrate.

4\. #emph[The practice of those who give the reins to grief, fear, wrath] and other like violent passions, so that by them they weaken their bodies and shorten their days.

5\. #emph[The practice of self-murderers,] who in this break the rule of love (“as yourself”)#footnote[Matt 22:39.] and end their days in a most horrible sin, depriving themselves of the time, place and means of repentance. So, whatever foolish excuse they make for their sin, little better can be thought of them than that they thrust their souls headlong into hell, unless the Lord, between the act done and their last breath, extraordinarily touches their hearts. Religion, nature, sense and all abhor this fearful deed. So not only those who have been enlightened #footnote[#emph[—Quam vellent aethere in alto, Nunc & pauperiem, & duros perferre labores. Virg. Aen.] 6. \[How gladly would they now, in the air above, bear both poverty and hard toils!\]] by God’s word, but also the heathen, who had no other light than the light of nature, have judged it to be a most desperate sin.

=== §. 64. #emph[Of haters of others.]

2\. By the affection which nature moves men to bear to their own flesh, we may see how nature prevails more with men than conscience and obedience to God’s word, yes, more than the #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] Spirit. For while nature keeps all men from hating their own flesh, nothing can keep many husbands from hating their wives, and wives their husbands; nor brothers, kinsmen and neighbors (yet these are “our own flesh”); no, nor many of those #footnote[Isa 58:7.] who profess themselves to be of the mystical body of Christ, from hating one another. What shall we say of these? Is nature of greater power, and more mighty in working, than the Spirit? Surely such men either deceive themselves and others in pretending to be members of the body of Christ, or else the Spirit is very weak in them, and the flesh bears great sway. Let haters of their brethren think on this and be ashamed.

=== §. 65. #emph[Of man’s care in providing and using things needful for his body.]

The second evidence of the love which a man bears to himself is noted in two such branches (#emph[nourishes] and #emph[cherishes]) as comprise #footnote[See §. 55.] all needful things under them. So the apostle implies by them that

#emph[Nature teaches all men to provide such things as are needful for] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[them:] needful for life, as food, and needful for health, as clothing. Nature is set forth here as a schoolmaster to Christians; therefore what nature teaches is a bounden duty. Solomon insists much on it, who in this respect #footnote[Eccl 2:24; 3:13; 5:18; 8:15.] says, “It is good and fitting … to eat and drink, and to find satisfaction in all the labor one does.”

If he who does not provide for his #footnote[1 Tim 5:8. #emph[Inconcussa naturae lege diligimus nos ipsos, quae in bestias etiam promulgata est. Aug. de Doct. Chr. li.] 1. #emph[ca.] 26. \[By an unshaken law of nature, which has been proclaimed even to the beasts, we love ourselves.\]] own is worse than an unbeliever, what is he who does not provide for himself? Even worse than a beast, for nature has taught the brute beasts to nourish and cherish themselves. If any think that this better befits beasts, or natural men, than saints, let them tell me which of the saints, guided by God’s Spirit, has at any time wholly neglected himself. To pass over all others, it is expressly noted of Christ that, as there was occasion, he #footnote[Matt 8:24.] slept, he #footnote[Luke 14:1.] ate, he #footnote[John 4:6.] rested, and otherwise refreshed himself.

#emph[Objection.] Though he was #footnote[John 4:31. #emph[etc.]] hungry, and food was prepared for him, yet he refused to eat.

#emph[Answer.] 1. Forgoing one meal is no great hindrance to #footnote[Lawful fasts are no hindrance to the cherishing of the body.] cherishing the body.

2\. Extraordinary and weighty occasions may lawfully make a man neglect himself a little, so that he may show that he prefers God’s glory and his brother’s salvation before the outward nourishing of his body. To this purpose Christ says, “My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me”; that is, I #footnote[John 4:34.] prefer it before my food. And Saint Paul says, “I will most #footnote[2 Cor 12:15.] gladly spend and be spent for the sake of your#super[+] souls.” We must therefore take heed here of the extremes on both sides.

1\. Of undue and excessive neglect of our bodies, so that their strength is wasted and their health impaired.

2\. Of caring too much for them, so that on no occasion will we lose a meal or a night’s rest. Fasting and keeping vigil, as occasion requires, are bounden duties.

But to return to the point of nourishing and cherishing our flesh:

1\. For this end God has provided food, clothing and all #footnote[1\. #emph[Reason.]] things needful for our weak bodies, that they should be nourished and cherished by them. Not to use them, therefore, is to refuse God’s providence.

2\. When we nourish and cherish our bodies well, they are the better enabled to do the work and service which God appoints to be done; but when we neglect them, they are disabled for it. As this is a motive, so it ought to be an end at which we aim in nourishing and cherishing our bodies.

=== §. 66. #emph[Of those who neglect to cherish their bodies.]

Many offend against this good instinct of nature. #footnote[Use.]

1\. #emph[Covetous misers,] who so dote on their wealth, and so #footnote[1\. Covetous misers.] delight in an abundance of goods stored up, that they do not allow themselves the things needful to nourish and cherish their bodies. Solomon rebukes such men much. Of them he says that there is “wealth #footnote[Eccl 2:23; 5:11, 12, 13, 14.] hoarded to the harm of its owner.” Daily experience gives evidence of its truth. For besides making their riches #footnote[1 Tim 6:9.] snares and #footnote[Mark 10:23, #emph[etc.]] hindrances that keep them from eternal life, such men make this present life very irksome, #footnote[Eccl 2:23.] filling their heads full of much gnawing care and keeping them from quiet rest. Many in this case are so besotted #footnote[Semper eget liquidis, semper abundat aquis. Ovid. in Ibin. \[He is always in want of drink, always surrounded by waters.\]] that, though they have abundance, they will not allow themselves in health a good meal or decent clothing, nor in sickness the medicine they need, no, nor a fire and such common things. Their case is worse than that of those who are in want: for others will pity and help such as are in want, but who will pity and help these?

2\. Those who are #emph[too intent] on their business, even the affairs #footnote[2\. They who are too intent on their calling.] of their lawful callings (for in good things there may be excess). Many students, preachers, lawyers, tradesmen, farmers, laborers and others offend in this, when they do not allow their bodies seasonable times of refreshment and rest, but fast, go without sleep and toil too much in their calling. Those who disable themselves by such means make themselves guilty of neglecting as much good as they might have done if they had nourished and cherished their bodies. Some are so eager in their business that they think all the time misspent which is spent in nourishing and cherishing their bodies, and so they wish that their bodies needed no food, sleep or other such means of refreshment. These thoughts and #footnote[Unlawful to desire that our bodies needed no refreshing.] desires are foolish and sinful in many respects, as

1\. In showing a secret discontent and grudging against God’s providence, for God has so ordered our condition for the clearer manifestation of man’s weakness and of God’s care over him.

2\. In taking away occasions of calling on God and giving him praise. For if we did not stand in such need of God’s providence, would we pray to him so often for his blessing? If we did not feel the sweetness and comfort of his providence by the good means which he affords us, would we be so thankful to him?

3\. In taking away the means of mutual love. For if, because of our weakness, we did not stand in need of help and relief from one another, what trial would there be of our love?

3\. Those who #emph[sever] these two duties of nature (#emph[nourishing] and #footnote[3\. They who make excess in one needful thing a hindrance to another.] #emph[cherishing]) and make them a hindrance to one another. Some so #emph[nourish] their bodies that they cannot #emph[cherish] them; that is, they spend so much on eating and drinking that they have nothing to clothe themselves with. Others so #emph[cherish] them that they cannot nourish them; that is, they so deck themselves out in fine clothing beyond their means that they do not have sufficient food for themselves. These fall into two contrary extremes: into #emph[excess] in one thing, and into #emph[defect] in another.

=== §. 67. #emph[Of contentment with what is sufficient.]

As the apostle, by naming these two (#emph[nourish, cherish]), shows that both of them are needful, so by naming them only, and no more than them, he shows that these two are sufficient. From this we learn that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Having food and clothing, we must be content with these.] #footnote[Doctrine.]]

The apostle lays down this doctrine in these very words in #footnote[1 Tim 6:8.] another place. The #footnote[Prov 30:8.] prayer of Agur and the tenor of the #footnote[Matt 6:11.] fourth petition prove as much.

#emph[Question.] Is a man then strictly bound to care for no more than food to nourish him and clothing to cherish him?

#emph[Answer.] Provided this nourishing and cherishing is extended to the condition in which God has set us, to the charge which God has given us, and to the calling which he has appointed for us, we ought to care for no more.

Let us therefore take heed of the excess which arises from the corruption of nature, and content ourselves with the sufficiency which nature requires.
